 and swift, gruesome death—Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and unshakable self-assurance, retreats with a thousand courtiers into a secluded, fortified abbey. This fortress, a marvel of architecture and artistry, is divided into seven uniquely themed rooms, each adorned with stained-glass windows that cast shifting, surreal light across the walls. The final chamber, however, is shrouded in darkness, its blood-red windows and a massive ebony clock that chimes each hour, halting all revelry with a chilling reminder of mortality. For five months, the revelers indulge in lavish masquerades, attempting to escape the dread beyond their walls, but the oppressive presence of the clock and the forbidden black room linger as unspoken anxieties.\\n\\nThe tension escalates during a grand masked ball in the fifth month of isolation. As the clock chimes, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, its form drenched in blood and masked like a corpse. The guests are horrified, their joy shattered by the grotesque intrusion. Prince Prospero, consumed by fury and a sense of personal affront, draws his dagger and pursues the figure through the seven rooms, each step echoing with the weight of his arrogance and denial. His pursuit is not merely a quest for justice but a desperate attempt to assert control over the inevitable, a final act of defiance against the plague he believed he could outwit.\\n\\nThe climax unfolds in the black chamber, where Prospero confronts the figure and collapses dead, only to discover it is empty—revealing the horrifying truth that the Red Death has infiltrated the sanctuary, not as an outsider, but as an inescapable force that cannot be barred by walls or wealth. In that moment, the illusion of safety shatters. One by one, the revelers fall, their lives extinguished as the clock stops and the lights fade into eternal darkness. The abbey, once a sanctuary of indulgence, becomes a tomb, its opulence now a mockery of human folly. The story ends not with redemption or survival, but with the complete triumph of death over pride, isolation, and illusion.\",\n      \"themes\": [\n        \"identity\",\n        \"ambition\",\n        \"loss\"\n      ],\n      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n    },\n    \"model_usage\": {\n      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Character Extraction\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Character Profiles\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"timing\": {\n      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n        \"duration_seconds\": 16.48,\n        \"duration_formatted\": \"16.5s\"\n      },\n      \"Character Extraction\": {\n        \"duration_seconds\": 295.14,\n        \"duration_formatted\": \"4m 55s\"\n      },\n      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n        \"duration_seconds\": 26.23,\n        \"duration_formatted\": \"26.2s\"\n      },\n      \"Character Profiles\": {\n        \"duration_seconds\": 13.82,\n        \"duration_formatted\": \"13.8s\"\n      },\n      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n        \"duration_seconds\": 71.5,\n        \"duration_formatted\": \"1m 12s\"\n      },\n      \"total\": {\n        \"duration_seconds\": 428.55,\n        \"duration_formatted\": \"7m 9s\"\n      },\n      \"started_at\": \"2026-01-19 01:45:21\",\n      \"ended_at\": \"2026-01-19 01:52:29\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"warnings\": [],\n  \"low_confidence_items\": [],\n  \"_analysis_metadata\": {\n    \"analyzed_at\": \"2026-01-19T01:52:39.588653\",\n    \"analyzer_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n    \"pip